From 45fdd4354347847ea8a46eb48fdaa87cde734217 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Kryxzort <101042137+Kryxzort@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 20 Nov 2026 00:11:40 +0530
Subject: [PATCH 134/135] render: a particle frame at an fps of 1 to 20 is two
 half steps, as in WE
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Wallpaper Engine 2.8.797's particle frame (0x140209a50) ages and kills the particles by the whole step, updates the
control points, runs the emitters, then the operator interpreter (0x1402132b0): at 0x14020a7a8 it reads the scene's
fps setting (int, ctx +0x150) and when it is 1 to 20 (`dec eax; cmp eax, 0x13; ja`) it halves the step and the
forces' step (0.5 at 0x1405784c8) and runs the interpreter twice; otherwise once. WE's default fps is 15, so every
particle system at the default rate steps this way. The interpreter's head copies the spawn size, alpha and colour
back before the operators multiply them, so each half starts from them afresh. The first half sets flag 0x4 at
system +0x3fe: only the interpreter's op 2, `movementpath` (allocated with type byte 2 at 0x1401e7db0 from the
parser's "movementpath" at 0x1401d78f4), reads it (0x1402137ba): a blend of 0.5 instead of 1 in how much of a
particle's offset from its path the step sheds. This core has no `movementpath` (nor does any scene of a 321-item
library), so nothing here reads it. After the steps the control points' positions are
kept as the last ones, once a frame.

The core ran the operators once a frame. They now run twice at an fps of 1 to 20 (the host's
wp_project_set_frame_rate, rounded), each half with dt / 2 and the forces' step / 2, with size, alpha and colour
reset before each half; the ageing, emitters, control points and children stay once a frame. What changes is what
isn't linear in the step: drag ((1 − d/2)² against 1 − d), gravity's integration (half a step's lag less), the
forces. A child system steps the same way (WE's children go through the same frame).

The frame's 0.1 s cap stays: WE caps it too (2.8.797's main loop: the measured frame time min (…, 0.1) at
0x1401272c1, the scene's step, rate and speed applied, min (…, 0.1) again at 0x14012745e), so the comment now says
so instead of "simulation instability".

Origin: ours
Why: docs/TODO.md (copy WE's frame-rate quirks: particles at the default fps)
Status: candidate-for-upstream
Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 .../Render/Objects/CParticle.cpp              | 41 +++++++++++++------
 1 file changed, 28 insertions(+), 13 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/core/WallpaperEngine/Render/Objects/CParticle.cpp b/src/core/WallpaperEngine/Render/Objects/CParticle.cpp
index b2ed030..5327f0a 100644
--- a/src/core/WallpaperEngine/Render/Objects/CParticle.cpp
+++ b/src/core/WallpaperEngine/Render/Objects/CParticle.cpp
@@ -569,8 +569,8 @@ void CParticle::render () {
     m_time = this->getContext ().getContext ().renderTime;
 
     if (dt > 0.0f) {
-	// Cap dt to prevent simulation instability
-	// Also provides more consistent behavior across different FPS
+	// WE caps a frame's step at 0.1 s too: the measured frame time, then the scene's step (rate and speed applied),
+	// each min (…, 0.1) (2.8.797: 0x1401272c1, 0x14012745e) before the scene and its systems step by it
 	dt = std::min (dt, 0.1f);
 	// the `rate` override is the system's playback speed: it scales the whole step (motion, ageing and
 	// emission alike), at least 0.01 (GH 0x140255d00: dt *= max(rate, 0.01)); read every frame
@@ -789,23 +789,38 @@ void CParticle::update (float dt) {
 	}
     }
 
-    // Update particle age; size, alpha and colour go back to their spawn values for the operators to multiply
-    // (the head of WE's operator interpreter, GH 0x140265800)
+    // Update particle age, once a frame by the whole step (2.8.797: before the interpreter, 0x140209a50)
     for (uint32_t i = 0; i < m_particleCount; i++) {
-	auto& p = m_particles[i];
-	p.age += dt;
-	p.size = p.initial.size;
-	p.alpha = p.initial.alpha;
-	p.color = p.initial.color;
+	m_particles[i].age += dt;
     }
 
+    // WE at an fps setting of 1 to 20 (its default 15) runs the operator interpreter twice a frame, each on half the
+    // step and half the forces' step (2.8.797: 0x14020a7a8, `dec eax; cmp eax, 0x13; ja` on its fps, then the
+    // interpreter 0x1402132b0 twice); the ageing, the emitters, the control points and the children stay once a frame.
+    // The first half's flag (0x4 at system +0x3fe) is read only by `movementpath` (interpreter op 2), which this core
+    // does not run. Halves change what isn't linear in the step: drag, gravity's integration, the forces.
+    // Guard: patches/lwe/tests host_api_test particle_half_steps_below_21_fps
+    const long fps = std::lround (getScene ().getFrameRate ());
+    const int halves = fps >= 1 && fps <= 20 ? 2 : 1;
+    const float stepDt = dt / static_cast<float> (halves);
+
     // the forces' step (the interpreter's third argument, GH 0x14025c2e0): dt · min (0.025 / frame time, 1)^0.7, so
     // drag and the forces weaken below 40 fps; the frame time is inferred to be the real one (WE: host +0x14c)
-    m_forceDt = dt * std::pow (std::min (0.025f / std::max (m_frameTime, 1e-4f), 1.0f), 0.7f);
+    m_forceDt = stepDt * std::pow (std::min (0.025f / std::max (m_frameTime, 1e-4f), 1.0f), 0.7f);
 
-    // Apply operators to living particles (including alphafade)
-    for (auto& op : m_operators) {
-	op (m_particles, m_particleCount, m_opControlPoints, m_simTime, dt);
+    for (int half = 0; half < halves; half++) {
+	// size, alpha and colour go back to their spawn values for the operators to multiply (the head of WE's
+	// operator interpreter, GH 0x140265800), so the second half multiplies them afresh, as WE's does
+	for (uint32_t i = 0; i < m_particleCount; i++) {
+	    auto& p = m_particles[i];
+	    p.size = p.initial.size;
+	    p.alpha = p.initial.alpha;
+	    p.color = p.initial.color;
+	}
+	// Apply operators to living particles (including alphafade)
+	for (auto& op : m_operators) {
+	    op (m_particles, m_particleCount, m_opControlPoints, m_simTime, stepDt);
+	}
     }
     for (int i = 0; i < 8; i++) {
 	m_controlPointLast[i] = m_opControlPoints[i].position;
